> Here is a one-pager on a proposal I am writing:
> ===
> Our understanding of climate risks remains siloed, with relatively independent literatures around process-based models, econometric approaches, and macroeconomic models. These parallel activities are no longer adequately addressing societal goals, particularly due to the rising importance of adaptation, and the need to better understand non-marginal changes in the economy and tipping points in the climate; cascading, compounding, and systemic risks; and the interactions between climate risks, adaptation, development, and mitigation.
> 
> This project proposed a set of interconnected approaches to address this challenge, applied to some of the most important and understudied climate risks:
> Asset and insurance losses from river and surface flooding, with the role of land use;
> The climate drivers of biodiversity loss and the potential and limits of conservation;
> The costs and benefits of actions to reduce annuals and perennials crop losses;
> An expanded understanding of GDP growth that accounts for international trade.
> 
> In the project, machine learning and computational Bayesian modeling are applied to these contexts and offer new opportunities to combine process-based and econometric approaches. Recent and planned improvements in macroeconomic models also play a crucial role.
> 
> These methodological innovations offer a firmer foundation for future risk estimates and a more flexible representation of adaptation. The work is built upon the understanding that process-based and econometric approaches are each inadequate, but have complementary strengths. Process-based models capture non-stationarities missed by statistical approaches, while econometrics parameterise a broader system response than process-based models.
> 
> Impact Channel
> Evidence Gap
> Methodological innovations
> Key Adaptation Dimension
> River & Surface Flooding
> Almost exclusively studied with process-based approaches
> Use of hydrological flow estimates; Hurdle model of EM-DAT; adaptation through spatial first-differences
> Land use and population growth (e.g., periurban margin in developing countries); flood control dams.
> Biodiversity Loss
> Abundance and suitability studies common, but local/global extinction threat a challenge
> Use Bayesian to calibrate risk profiles by species, assuming weather shocks drive local collapses.
> Protected areas, and the role of cross-boundary benefits of protection.
> Annual & Perennial Crops
> Perennial crops are understudied, due to lack of data on management choices
> Combining process-based outputs with econometric models; hidden variable Bayes model
> Irrigation, fertiliser, weather protection, capping, replanting.
> GDP Loss
> Econometric estimates ignore the role of foreign shocks
> Combine machine learning with trade networks to evaluate a multi-country impacts
> Changing management of supply chains.
> ===
