> Here is some more intro text, which might help constrain the acronym:
> ===
> Introduction
> 
> The impacts of climate change are incredibly complex. The human and natural systems of the future may be unrecognisable to economists and scientists today, and their evolution will be partly shaped by how they are impacted by and respond to climate risk. Impacts emerge from local interactions, long-term climate trends, and unexpected weather shocks, while the globally correlated nature of climate change disrupts systems at all scales. Climate risks interact across space, time, and sectors, resulting in cascading, concurrent, and systemic risks.
> 
> The scientific methods that have emerged to understand climate risks struggle with this task. Biophysical process models, with long histories in areas like agriculture (CITE) and flooding (CITE), have had significant problems when used to project historical losses (CITE). Econometric statistical approaches have emerged in many areas, such as health (CITE) and economic growth (CITE), but these assume a level of stationarity that is difficult to justify (CITE). Macroeconomic models have played an important role to synthesise impacts across the economy (CITE), but these assume that impacts do not result in structural changes (CITE).
> 
> At the same time, these different methods have complementary strengths: Biophysical process models capture non-stationarities missed by statistical approaches (CITE), likely to be important under future projections, while econometrics provides accurate estimates of currently attributable climate risks (CITE). Biophysical process models typically include management-based adaptation; econometric models capture autonomous adaptation; and macroeconomic models capture economy-wide shifts. Importantly, these three models operate at different, nested scales.
> 
> while econometrics parameterise a broader system response than process-based models (Franziska et al. 2021). 
> 
> This project focuses on the "glue" that will support a better understanding of climate risks by integrating multiple approaches. This integration is made possible by new methods in  machine learning, artificial intelligence, computational Bayesian methods, and open-source frameworks. The project proposes to study four types of impacts, and to break ground with a better understanding of adaptation in each one. The projects are selected to build-out, across their range, a set of methods that are applicable to a broad set of impacts.
> ===
